The integral of $f(x)^2$ is minimized when $f(x)$ is as "flat" as possible, which is a constant function.  We can prove this using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.

By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
\left(\int_0^2 f(x) \,dx\right)^2 &\leq \left(\int_0^2 (f(x))^2 \,dx\right)\left(\int_0^2 1^2 \,dx\right)\\
\Rightarrow\qquad 5^2 &\leq \left(\int_0^2 (f(x))^2 \,dx\right)\cdot 2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{25}{2} &\leq \int_0^2 (f(x))^2 \,dx.
\end{align*}

Thus, the minimum value of the integral is $\frac{25{2}}$.
